\chapter{References and shared cells}
A \api{ref} declaration aliases an existing mutable binding. It does not copy
the current value and does not create a pointer value in the language. Both
names refer to the same cell in an environment.

\begin{mullcode}
var count <- 4;
ref alias <- count;
alias <- alias + 1;
G:Assert(count == 5);
count <- 9;
G:Assert(alias == 9);
val snapshot <- count;
alias <- 12;
G:Assert(snapshot == 9);
\end{mullcode}

The initializer of \api{ref} must be a name resolving to a mutable cell.
An arbitrary expression, a list index, or an immutable binding is rejected.
An alias to another reference is allowed because the latter already denotes
the same mutable cell. Assignment through either name checks the cell's
mutability.

\section{References in closures}
An alias can be captured by a function, and the cell remains available while
the runtime retains the closure's environment. This is useful for shared
counters or a replaceable host callback. Function parameters are ordinary
mutable local bindings initialized with argument values; MuLL does not have
reference-parameter syntax. Capture an existing cell when shared mutation is
the intended interface.

Lists and records remain structurally immutable even when reached through a
reference. A reference permits replacing the entire stored value. This avoids
hidden element mutation through a copied collection while still supporting
explicit shared state through named cells.
